# fixedbugs, panel 042, 2026-08-12.
#
# SYMPTOM: `heroes run` on this file answered `internal error: compiling the
# generated C failed … error: invalid argument type 'char *' to unary
# expression`, and exited **2**. Under CLAUDE.md §7 exit 2 means the *compiler*
# is wrong. It is not: `getenv` returns `char *`, the declaration below says
# `i64`, and that is an ordinary author mistake — arguably the single most
# likely wrong FFI return type there is, because a C function returning a
# string is the first thing anybody binds.
#
# CAUSE: `emit/externs.rs` wrote the return assertion as `_Generic(+(c), …)`.
# Unary `+` applied to a pointer is a **hard clang error**, not a failed
# assertion, so compilation died before the marker string
# `heroes-ffi-return getenv i64` was ever produced — and `emit/ffi.rs` matches
# on exactly that marker to decide that a clang failure is §7's named
# exception. The mechanism panel 036 built to say "your declaration disagrees
# with the header" was unreachable for the return type most likely to disagree.
#
# FIX: the `+` is deleted. It was one of two changes made together at
# M-ffi-ladder to accept enum-returning C functions, and measurement showed the
# other one — widening the accepted set to `unsigned i64` and its narrower
# siblings — was sufficient on its own, because C11 6.5.1.1 selects an enum's
# compatible integer type. See `an_enum_returning_extern_needs_no_unary_plus`,
# which is the test that fires if that premise ever comes back.
#
# This file must NOT compile. It is built by the CLI surface test
# `a_wrong_pointer_return_is_a_diagnostic_not_an_internal_error`.

extern "stdlib.h"
    function getenv(name: cstr) -> i64

function main()
    print(getenv("PATH".cstr()))
